class:: Loudness2
summary:: Calibrated revision of the informal SuperCollider loudness model
categories:: UGens>Analysis>MIR
related:: Guides/MIR2, Classes/Loudness

description::
Returns [sones, phons] using a modernization of Nick Collins's 2007 SuperCollider loudness model and its 42-band equal-loudness contour table. Source: link::https://github.com/supercollider/supercollider/blob/Version-3.14.1/server/plugins/Loudness.cpp::. This is an informal perceptual feature, not an ISO-compliant or EBU R128 meter. The original contour table does not document its acoustic-field provenance; MIR2 applies no diffuse-field or free-field correction. Absolute acoustic claims require externally calibrated and equalized input and independent validation.

Band boundaries are stored in Hz from the original 44.1 kHz/1024 grid; each FFT bin contributes according to its overlap with a band. One-sided spectral power is converted to mean-square input power with Parseval scaling and the specified windowPower. A unit-peak sine corresponds to calibration dB SPL. Per-band SPL is interpolated through the inherited contour table, whose per-band range is 2..100 phon; lower levels contribute nothing and higher ones saturate at 100 phon. Intensities 10.pow(phon/10)-1 are summed, giving total phons = 10*log10(1+sum). The subtraction removes the legacy model's residual silence.

After exponential attack/release smoothing of summed excitation, sones = 2.pow((phons-40)/10) above 40 phon. Below 40 phon, sones = (phons/40).pow(2.86), reaching zero at silence. Attack and release are e-folding time constants in seconds, measured using actual time between FFT frames. At zero smoothing, silence returns [0, 0] immediately; with release smoothing the previous excitation decays to zero. The model excludes DC and frequencies below about 21.5 Hz, and its table ends near 22.05 kHz.

Memory is one magnitude frame. Cost is linear in FFT bins plus the 42-band contour conversions; no allocations occur during processing. It adds smoothing delay but no lookahead beyond the FFT window. A 1024-sample FFT window is 21.3 ms at 48 kHz.

Like vanilla link::Classes/Loudness::, this estimates informal perceptual loudness in sones. Loudness2 supports explicit FFT size and window/calibration conventions, returns phons too, removes residual silence, and expresses smoothing in seconds. It omits the legacy per-bin spectral masking heuristic; its levels are therefore not a drop-in numerical match.

classmethods::
method:: kr
argument:: chain
FFT chain whose buffer matches fftSize.
argument:: calibration
SPL assigned to a unit-peak sinusoid, clipped to 20..140 dB; default 100. This is a declared input convention, not an automatic microphone calibration.
argument:: attack
Excitation attack time constant in seconds; 0 disables attack smoothing. Default 0.01.
argument:: release
Excitation release time constant in seconds; 0 disables release smoothing. Default 0.1.
argument:: windowPower
Mean square of the FFT analysis window. Default 0.375 matches a full-length Hann window; use 1 for a rectangular window. Other windows and zero padding require their actual energy gain over fftSize.
argument:: fftSize
Literal power of two 256..8192, matching the FFT buffer.
returns::
Two control outputs: sones, then phons. Their accuracy is limited by the inherited informal model.

examples::
Loudness2 models human perceptual loudness in sones using equal-loudness contours. In this example, it drives an opto-style feedback compressor: perceptual sones regulate smooth, non-linear gain attenuation, naturally avoiding the pumping artifacts typical of simple peak or RMS compressors on bass-heavy drums. An interactive code::MIR2GUI.scope:: displays real-time perceived loudness in sones.
code::
(
s.waitForBoot({
    var path = MIR2GUI.soundPath("drums.wav");
    ~mir2Buffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~mir2Bus = Bus.control(s, 1);
    s.sync;
    ~mir2Example = {
        var source = if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) {
            PlayBuf.ar(1, ~mir2Buffer, BufRateScale.kr(~mir2Buffer), loop: 1)
        } {
            // Synthetic drum fallback
            var trig = Impulse.kr(2);
            var kick = SinOsc.ar(EnvGen.ar(Env([120, 45], [0.08], \exp), trig)) * EnvGen.ar(Env.perc(0.005, 0.3), trig);
            var snare = WhiteNoise.ar * EnvGen.ar(Env.perc(0.005, 0.15), Impulse.kr(2, 0.5));
            (kick + snare) * 0.5
        };
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var chain = FFT(LocalBuf(1024), source, hop: 0.5);
        var loudness = Loudness2.kr(chain, calibration: 100, attack: 0.005, release: 0.08, fftSize: 1024);
        var sones = loudness[0];

        // Opto-style non-linear gain reduction driven by sones
        var threshold = 12.0;
        var excess = (sones - threshold).max(0);
        var gainReduction = (1.0 / (1.0 + (excess * 0.15))).lag(0.04);
        var compressed = source * gainReduction;
        var sound = compressed * 0.35;

        if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { Out.kr(~mir2Bus, sones) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2) ! 2
    }.play;

    ~mir2GUI = MIR2GUI.scope("Perceived Loudness", ~mir2Bus, 0.0, 50.0, "sones");
});
)
::
code::
(
~mir2Example.free;
if(~mir2GUI.notNil) { ~mir2GUI.close };
if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { ~mir2Bus.free };
if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) { ~mir2Buffer.free };
)
::

